# A .dockerignore pattern is matched against the WHOLE path, with `*` stopping
# at `/` - it is not a .gitignore. So `*.log` alone covers the repository root
# and nothing else, and every artifact that a build or a local run drops deeper
# in the tree has to be spelled out with `**/`. Each one that is not excluded
# goes into the build context, and any change to it invalidates the layer that
# copies its directory - see the header of `Dockerfile` and
# `scripts/test-docker-build-cache.sh`.
**/target/
target-*/
.git/
.fm-secondmate/
venv/
.venv/
models*/
source-models*/
tests/
*.gguf
*.part
# A node started from the checkout writes `infer_server_<pid>.log` next to
# `crates/node`. Those files are in .gitignore, so nobody sees them, and each
# new one used to re-run the whole Rust build.
**/*.log
*.key
pom.json
pom.db
pom.db-*
docs/superpowers/
**/node_modules/
apps/frontend/dist*
# Written by `npm run build|dev|test` on the host. The image regenerates all of
# them (`prebuild` runs build-brand.mjs and build-docs.mjs), and a `tsbuildinfo`
# from the host is worse than useless here: it records the host's paths and
# timestamps, and `tsc -b` would trust it.
**/*.tsbuildinfo
apps/frontend/src/brand/
apps/frontend/src/docs/
apps/frontend/public/brand/
# The fork's own history - the largest single item in the context (396 MB on
# salem) and one that changes on every fetch in it. Nothing reads it: the
# builder image has no `git`, and llama.cpp's `cmake/git-vars.cmake` only runs
# git when `find_package(Git)` succeeds. The fork cache identity reaches the
# build as `LLAMA_CPP_CACHE_KEY`, computed on the host by
# `scripts/build-docker.sh`.
llama.cpp/.git/
llama.cpp/build/
llama.cpp/build-*/
